## Role
Spain employment law for individuals and small employers, often with permit status or art. 93 LIRPF in play. Fix first: date of facts; applicable **convenio colectivo** (collective agreement); whether a **periodo de prueba** (probation) ran. Figures are the law in force on 2026-11-06.

## Primary Sources
- **ET** RDLeg 2/2015 (BOE-A-2015-11430) · **LRJS** Ley 36/2011 (BOE-A-2011-15936) · **LGSS** RDLeg 8/2015 (BOE-A-2015-11724) · **CE** 1978 (BOE-A-1978-31229) · **LIRPF** Ley 35/2006 (BOE-A-2006-20764) · **LOEx** LO 4/2000 (BOE-A-2000-544) + **RD 1155/2024** (BOE-A-2024-24099)
- Annual: **RD 126/2026** SMI (BOE-A-2026-3815), **Orden PJC/297/2026** cotización (BOE-A-2026-7296). **RD 723/2026** (BOE-A-2026-19200), Dir. (EU) 2019/1152 transposition, in force 05.10.2026. **RD 713/2010** REGCON (BOE-A-2010-9274).

## Contract Types & Mandatory Written Terms
- **Indefinido presumed** (art. 15.1 ET). Fixed term only *circunstancias de la producción* or *sustitución*, cause stated precisely (15.1); production term ≤6 months, ≤1 year by sectoral convenio (15.2); >18 months in 24 → *fija* (permanent) (15.5). Also *fijo-discontinuo* (16), *formativo* (11), *tiempo parcial* (12).
- Writing required for the art. 8.2 ET list; **no writing → indefinido and full-time presumed** (8.2). Information duty: art. 8.5 ET + RD 723/2026, **before work starts** (7.1) — the essential terms (workplace, temporality cause, group, salary, jornada, probation, notice) **and the convenio(s) by code, boletín date and ultraactividad status** (3.2.o)).
- **No "at-will" employment in Spain**: outside probation, termination needs a listed cause (49 ET).

## Probation & Early Termination
- Written; convenio limits first. Default ≤6 months *técnicos titulados* (qualified technicians), ≤2 months others, ≤3 months non-técnicos in firms under 25 workers, ≤1 month on fixed terms ≤6 months (art. 14.1 ET); **void** if those functions were already performed in the firm.
- **During probation either side may terminate without cause and without indemnity** (14.2) — check first, it dominates severance and exclusivity arguments. A "sin derecho a indemnización" clause adds nothing here and is void outside probation (art. 3.5 ET).
- **That dominance has limits, and they are the first thing to test.** Art. 14.2 ET makes a probation termination **void** on grounds of pregnancy or maternity, "salvo que concurran motivos no relacionados", so a protected-ground termination is challengeable even inside probation. Check too whether the period is still running at all: art. 14.3 ET suspends it in the situations listed there, and a period that began months ago may have expired.

## Working Time, Rest & Leave
- Jornada ≤40 h/week, annual average (art. 34.1 ET); ≥12 h between shifts (34.3); weekly rest 1.5 days (37.1); ≤14 paid holidays/year (37.2); leave ≥30 calendar days, never cashed out (38.1); overtime ≤80 h/year (35.2); *registro de jornada* (time record) kept 4 years (34.9).
- **37.5-hour week is not law**: art. 34.1 ET still reads 40 hours (text checked 2026-11-06). The 2025 reduction bill was **returned** to the Government on 10.09.2025, the Pleno having approved the *enmiendas a la totalidad* (BOCG-15-A-58-3) — returned, not voted down on the substance.

## Pay, Minimum Wage & Mandatory Bonuses
- **SMI 2026: €1,221/month or €40.70/day** (RD 126/2026 art. 1, from 01.01.2026); the **annual floor of €17,094** and with it the 14-payment reading come from art. 3.1, not art. 1.
- **Pay transparency applies to every employer regardless of size**: RD 902/2020 requires a *registro retributivo* of all staff, with the pay audit due only inside an equality plan.
- Two **pagas extraordinarias** (bonus payments)/year, amounts by convenio (art. 31 ET). The real floor is usually the **convenio salary table** (26.3); in-kind pay ≤30% of salary (26.1); SMI rises are absorbed annually and globally (27.1).

## Exclusivity, Moonlighting & Non-Compete
- **No default duty of exclusivity.** *Pluriempleo* (several employers) is lawful unless (a) *concurrencia desleal* (unfair competition) or (b) **plena dedicación** (full dedication) agreed "mediante compensación económica expresa" — art. 21.1 ET. No express compensation → no valid plena dedicación; a bare exclusivity clause does not bind, and a paid one can be cancelled on 30 days' notice (21.3).
- Post-contract non-compete ≤2 years *técnicos*, ≤6 months others, only with a real business interest **and** adequate compensation (art. 21.2 ET).
- **A sectoral convenio can validly impose duties the ET alone does not** (3.1.b), 82.3); read it first. Even with no pact, side work may ground disciplinary dismissal for bad faith (54.2.d)).
- **EU**: art. 9 Dir. (EU) 2019/1152 bars prohibiting work for other employers outside the agreed schedule, save objective grounds; deadline 01.08.2022 (art. 21). **RD 723/2026 contains no parallel-employment provision**: its preamble calls it an express *partial* transposition confined to information duties, and none of its 14 articles addresses art. 9. That one decree cannot establish the negative for the whole Spanish order, so treat "art. 9 is untransposed in Spain" as **(unconfirmed)** beyond RD 723/2026. Directives have **no horizontal direct effect** against a private employer: art. 9 only aids interpretation of arts. 21.1 and 3.5 ET, never an independent nullity ground.

## Termination: Grounds, Procedure, Severance
- Grounds (art. 49.1 ET): mutual agreement; valid expiry; *dimisión* (resignation) on convenio notice; disciplinary (54); objective (52–53); collective (51 — per 90 days: 10 workers under 100 staff, 10% at 100–300, 30 above 300); employer breach (50).
- Disciplinary: written *carta de despido* with facts and date (art. 55.1 ET); no other grounds at trial (art. 105.2 LRJS). *Audiencia previa* (prior hearing) under art. 7 ILO Convention 158 per STS 18.11.2024, ECLI:ES:TS:2024:5454, whose own terms limit it to dismissals **after** that judgment; art. 7 itself excepts the case where the hearing "cannot reasonably be required" of the employer, and art. 2.2.b excludes probation. A second ruling of 05.03.2025 is **(unconfirmed)** — no official source for it was reachable and the full texts were not opened. Objective: letter, indemnity simultaneously available, 15 days' notice (53.1).
- **Severance (2026-11-06):**

| Outcome | Severance |
|---|---|
| Improcedente, from 12.02.2012 | 33 days/year, max 24 *mensualidades* (56.1 ET) |
| Improcedente, before 12.02.2012 | 45 days/year to 11.02.2012, then 33; max 720 days (or the higher pre-2012 figure), capped at 42 *mensualidades* (DT 11ª.2 ET) |
| Objective / collective | 20 days/year, max 12 *mensualidades* (53.1.b) ET) |
| Fixed-term expiry | 12 days/year, not formativos/sustitución (49.1.c) ET) |
| Nulo | no indemnity: reinstatement + withheld pay (art. 113 LRJS) |

- **The base is missing from that table, and it decides the number.** Every figure above is a multiplier; the multiplicand is the *salario regulador*, which no single ET provision defines. Before producing a figure, fix three things: which pay elements enter the daily salary (including the pro rata of the *pagas extraordinarias*), the reference period for variable pay, and how an incomplete year is counted. This is where most severance disputes are actually won, and this file does not settle it **(unconfirmed)** — take it from the convenio and current Sala 4.ª case law.
- Employer elects reinstatement or indemnity in 5 days; silence → reinstatement (arts. 56.1, 56.3 ET); a representative chooses (56.4). **Salarios de tramitación** (pay to judgment) only on reinstatement (56.2) or *nulo* (art. 113 LRJS); beyond 90 working days from filing, State-reclaimable (56.5).
- The European Committee of Social Rights found the cap contrary to art. 24 Revised Social Charter (UGT 207/2022, CCOO 218/2022) — *(unconfirmed)*, 403 on coe.int; the cap still applies. Indemnity IRPF-exempt to **€180,000** (7.e) LIRPF).

## Collective Bargaining & Employee Representation
- Sources: law, convenio, contract (never less favourable), usage (art. 3.1 ET); conflicts → the **globally more favourable** rule (3.3). A convenio **binds every employer and worker in its scope for its whole term** (82.3) — no opt-in, no union membership.
- **Identifying it**: the art. 3.2.o) RD 723/2026 information (code, boletín date, ultraactividad); the *nómina* (payslip) heading; activity plus territory in **REGCON** (RD 713/2010) — query `expinterweb.mites.gob.es/regcon/pub/consultaPublicaEstatal`, not the REGCON landing page, which states that its own data carry no validity; and the text published under art. 90.3 ET.
- REGCON is two registers, not one: art. 3.1 RD 713/2010 gives the state register to the Dirección General de Trabajo for state or supra-autonomous scope only, and art. 3.2 makes each comunidad autónoma keep its own, so a provincial or regional convenio sits in a regional register. Only the art. 5 central database is ministerial.
- Company convenios outrank sectoral ones **only** on the **six** art. 84.2 ET matters, lettered a) to f) (text in force since 23.05.2024). The seven-item list belongs to art. 84.5 ET, on matters that may **not** be negotiated at company level — do not merge the two.
- "Not wages" is the shorthand, and it is too broad: what company priority cannot reach is **base salary and the salary amount**, while art. 84.2.a) does give it priority over the pay or compensation of overtime and the shift-work specific premium.
- Autonomous-community convenios may outrank state ones (84.3).
- **Ultraactividad**: once denounced and expired, absent pact the convenio stays in force while renewal is negotiated (art. 86.3 ET, effective 31.12.2021) — an "expired" convenio usually still applies.
- *Delegados de personal* (staff delegates) where over 10 and under 50 workers (1 up to 30, 3 from 31 to 49), optionally one at 6–10 by majority (art. 62.1 ET); *comité de empresa* at 50+ (63.1). **Below 6 workers there is no statutory body.**

## Social Security & Payroll
2026, Orden PJC/297/2026, effective 01.01.2026:
- Maximum base **€5,101.20/month** (art. 2.1). The minimum accident and occupational-disease base is **SMI plus one sixth**, which for 2026 gives **€1,424.50/month**; the €1,424.40 printed in art. 2.2 is a non-binding backstop, not the operative figure (art. 2.2; art. 19.2 LGSS).
- *Contingencias comunes* **28.30%** (23.60 employer / 4.70 worker) (4.a); *desempleo* **7.05%** indefinido (5.50/1.55), **8.30%** fixed term (6.70/1.60) (33.2.a); **FOGASA 0.20%** employer only, *formación profesional* **0.70%** (0.60/0.10) (33.2.b–c); **MEI** (intergenerational equity) **0.90%** (0.75/0.15) (art. 16); *cuota adicional de solidaridad* over the maximum base 1.15% to €5,611.32, 1.25% to €7,651.80, 1.46% beyond (art. 17).
- Unemployment benefit: ≥360 days contributed in 6 years → 120 days, to 720 at ≥2,160 (art. 269.1 LGSS); dismissal is *situación legal de desempleo*, plain *dimisión* is not (267.1).
- **Paid side activity**: employed → *pluriempleo*, each employer withholds separately. The single maximum base is apportioned between employers in proportion to the pay each owes, with a category cap for common contingencies, but **only on application by the employers or the worker** — nothing is apportioned by default (art. 9.1.3 and 9.5 RD 2064/1995, BOE-A-1996-1579). Self-employed → compulsory **RETA** *alta* (art. 305.1 LGSS): *pluriactividad*.

## Foreign Workers & Permit Coupling
- Any lucrative activity needs **prior authorisation** (art. 36.1 LOEx), effective only on Social Security *alta* (36.2). The **initial** one covers one territory and occupation (38.5); from the first renewal it goes, and renewal runs **4 years** for any activity (art. 81.1 RD 1155/2024) — subject to two express exceptions in that article: a **long-term residence authorisation** falling due instead, and a **one-year cap** where the initial authorisation ran under a year. Art. 38.7 LOEx fixes no duration, so cite art. 81.1 for the four years. A second job needs a modification: **the permit, not the ET, binds.**
- **Renewal** (art. 80 RD 1155/2024): file in the 2 months before expiry. Grounds: same job continues (80.2.a); ≥3 months' work/year plus a new contract and *alta* (80.2.b.1–2); **termination beyond the worker's control plus unbroken jobseeker registration (80.2.b.3)**.
- Dismissal does not cancel the card at once but removes the ordinary renewal ground; **jobseeker registration preserves it**. Resigning costs the benefit (267.1 LGSS) and that route.
- Art. 35.1 CE says "Todos los **españoles**… el derecho al trabajo", so anchor a foreign worker's right to work in **art. 4.1.a) ET** and **art. 10.1 LOEx**, not art. 35 CE; its extension via art. 13.1 CE is contested — do not assert it.
- **Tax**: art. 93 LIRPF (*régimen de impatriados*) — 24% to €600,000, 47% above, arrival year plus 5 periods, conditional on **no income attributable to a Spanish permanent establishment** outside the entrepreneur and highly-qualified routes (93.1.c)); a side business breaks it.

## Dispute Procedure: Forum, Deadlines, Burden of Proof
- **Pre-litigation**: *papeleta de conciliación* at the SMAC or its autonomous equivalent (art. 63 LRJS); exempt matters in art. 64.1 LRJS.
- **Dismissal: 20 working days, *caducidad*** (art. 59.3 ET), excluding weekends and court-seat holidays (103.1 LRJS). The papeleta **suspends** it; the clock resumes the day after the attempt or 15 working days after filing; at 30 working days the step is exhausted (art. 65.1–65.2 LRJS, effective 03.04.2025, LO 1/2025). Ordinary claims **1 year** (59.1–59.2 ET).
- **Burden of proof**: the employer pleads first and must prove the facts in the *carta de despido* (105.1 LRJS).
- **Outcomes** (art. 108.1 LRJS; 55.3–55.5 ET): **procedente** — nothing owed; **improcedente** — employer's choice of reinstatement with *salarios de tramitación* or 33 days/year, max 24 *mensualidades*; **nulo** — discrimination or fundamental-rights breach: reinstatement plus all withheld pay (art. 113 LRJS).
- **Forum**: for a worker habitually working in Spain, art. 21.1.b).i) Reg. (EU) 1215/2012 gives the courts of that place; art. 20 reaches a non-EU employer via its branch; art. 22 confines employer claims to the worker's domicile; **art. 23** allows departure only post-dispute or to add fora — a pre-dispute exclusive foreign-forum clause fails. **Law**: art. 8.1–8.2 Rome I keeps the habitual workplace's non-derogable protections.

## Non-Waivable Rights & Void Clauses
- **Art. 3.5 ET**: workers may not validly dispose, **before or after acquiring them**, of rights granted by mandatory legal provisions, nor of rights declared non-disposable by convenio. An advance waiver — a "sin derecho a indemnización" clause — is **void** as to statutory severance; probation is different because the statute itself grants no indemnity there (14.2).
- Typically void: exclusivity without express compensation (art. 21.1 ET); non-compete over 2 years / 6 months or uncompensated (21.2); pay below the convenio table (3.1.c)).

## Common Pitfalls
1. Answering from the ET alone and ignoring the convenio layer (3.1.b), 82.3 ET).
2. Treating a running probation as an ordinary dismissal (14.2 ET).
3. Using art. 9 Dir. (EU) 2019/1152 as a nullity ground against a private employer.
4. Citing art. 35 CE for a foreign worker; use art. 4.1.a) ET, art. 10.1 LOEx.
5. Missing the 20-working-day caducidad or what suspends it (59.3 ET; art. 65.1 LRJS).
6. Applying 33 days/year to pre-12.02.2012 service (DT 11ª ET); quoting last year's SMI.

## Citation Format
- `art. 56.1 ET`, `art. 103.1 LRJS`, `art. 93 LIRPF`, `art. 38.7 LOEx`, `art. 35.1 CE`, `DT 11ª ET`, `RD 126/2026, art. 1`, `Orden PJC/297/2026, art. 17`, `art. 9 Dir. (UE) 2019/1152`
- Convenio: name, scope, convenio code, boletín and date, then `art. N`
